## Interface: 16001
## Title: Efficient Quests
## Notes: A step-by-step leveling guide for the Forever client.
## Version: 0.3.0
## OptionalDeps: FlareUI, TomTom, QuestieDB
## SavedVariables: EfficientQuestsDB
## SavedVariablesPerCharacter: EfficientQuestsCharacter

# The embedded libraries load first, in dependency order: HereBeDragons
# needs LibStub and CallbackHandler. The pins part of HereBeDragons is
# not embedded, because pins draw on maps.
Libs/LibStub/LibStub.lua
Libs/CallbackHandler-1.0/CallbackHandler-1.0.lua
Libs/HereBeDragons/HereBeDragons-2.0.lua
Core.lua

# The quest state reader loads right after the entry point, before every
# module that asks it questions or subscribes to its callbacks.
QuestState.lua

CharacterStore.lua

# The generated data tables copied verbatim from Trailwright (see
# THIRD_PARTY.md): the quest data, the Path, and the dungeon guide stops.
# They are plain data, so they load before any code that reads them.
QuestData.lua
PathData.lua
DungeonGuides.lua

# The data store checks the shape of the tables above at load time and
# exposes the data snapshot identity for the status command.
DataStore.lua

# The route planner walks the Path from the data store and the quest state
# and answers the route the step model and the window show. It also wires
# the quest state callbacks to the character stores.
RoutePlanner.lua

# The step model turns a route entry into a step -- plain data for the
# step window -- and answers the step of one quest for the Follow mode
# controller. It holds no frames; the window renders what it answers.
StepModel.lua

# The Follow mode controller listens for the quest the player picks in
# the quest log -- through the client's super tracking when it is there --
# and serves the step of that quest through the step model. It loads
# after the step model it asks; the step window polls it.
FollowMode.lua

# The QuestieDB fallback appends its lookups after the data store's own,
# so the store always wins and QuestieDB answers only what the store
# lacks. It loads last of the lookup modules, and its missing-data
# notices sit at the end of every lookup chain.
QuestieFallback.lua

# The options panel is a window of our own; it opens from the step window
# and from the /eq command, so it builds itself only on first open.
Options.lua

# The menu opens beside the step window, which hands itself over as the
# anchor of Menu.Open. Without an anchor it sits at a fixed screen spot.
Menu.lua

# Interface.lua is the old window module. It stays in this folder as a
# frozen reference for the new step window and is never loaded.
